Archive the three BandAMP forum citations as standalone pages

`docs/luckysevens.html` cites three threads on `forum.bandamp.com`. The forum is
being revamped and those URLs no longer resolve, so as of now the exhibit's three
sourced claims point at nothing. The posts are Autumn's own, from ~2005 — the age
is the point of the citation, not an incidental detail — so the fix is to hold a
renderable copy rather than to soften the claim or drop the link.

## What this adds

`tools/wayback-standalone` — pulls a page out of the Internet Archive *with its
stylesheets and images* and rewrites every asset reference to a relative path, so
the result renders offline out of one folder with no network and no dependence on
archive.org staying up.

`docs/library/lucky-sevens/citations/<slug>/` — one folder per cited thread:
`index.html`, `assets/`, and `provenance.json`.

## What makes it a citation rather than a screenshot

- Fetched through the archive's `id_` modifier, which returns the **original
  bytes** the server sent — no Wayback toolbar, no rewritten URLs.
- Assets are fetched **at the page's own capture timestamp**, so a 2008 page gets
  the 2008 stylesheet rather than whatever the archive holds from 2019.
- **The document body is not edited.** Asset references are repointed and nothing
  else. Provenance lives in an HTML comment and `provenance.json` — source URL,
  capture timestamp, retrieval time, every capture the archive holds, and an
  explicit list of assets it could not supply. A citation that silently rewrites
  its source is not a citation.
- `<a href>` links are deliberately **left pointing at the original addresses**.
  Those addresses are part of what is being cited.
- `#id40623` on the Audio_Review link is a fragment, resolved client-side, so it
  survives untouched.

## Captures used

| thread | capture | page | assets | missing |
| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| `The_Pit/14556.html` | 2025-07-15 13:26 UTC | 46 KB | 44 | 1 |
| `Feedback/5186.html` | 2008-07-06 06:18 UTC | 6.6 KB | 33 | 0 |
| `Audio_Review/3861.html` | 2024-04-18 03:38 UTC | 29 KB | 53 | 4 |

All three render offline with their period stylesheets. Zero references to
`web.archive.org` survive in any of the three documents, and `#id40623` on the
Audio_Review page is intact. The five missing assets are 404s in the archive
itself — Google Analytics' `urchin.js` and a handful of user avatars that were
already gone when the capture was taken; the tool records each one by URL in
`provenance.json` rather than silently rendering a broken page.

1.3 MB, 136 files.

Available captures, from the archive's CDX index:

| thread | captures |
| --- | --- |
| `The_Pit/14556.html` | 2019-07-22, 2019-11-14, 2021-04-20, **2025-07-15** |
| `Feedback/5186.html` | 2007-10-17, **2008-07-06** |
| `Audio_Review/3861.html` | 2007-01-10, 2007-10-16, 2008-07-20, 2009-10-10, 2019-12-10, 2020-09-26, **2024-04-18** |

Default is the newest capture: the site was healthiest most recently and later
captures carry more of the thread. `Feedback/5186` has only two, both from the
2000s. If an earlier skin reads better as period evidence, `--timestamp` takes any
row above and the tool will rebuild that one instead — it is one command per page,
not a re-derivation.

## The archive fights back, and the tool is built for it

The playback endpoint banned this IP for **roughly three hours** on the first
attempt, while the CDX index answered instantly throughout — so a run looks
broken when it is only throttled, and the throttle is far longer than any
reasonable first guess. The ban is path-scoped: `/web/` is refused, `/cdx/` is
not. It also compounds, so the cheapest way to avoid it is to not probe
impatiently before the real run, which is exactly what happened here.

Two escape routes were tested and are closed. `archive.today` requires a CAPTCHA.
The CDX API can in principle return the WARC `filename`/`offset`/`length` for a
capture, which would let you pull the bytes from `archive.org/download` — a
different host with a different rate limiter — but those fields are now redacted
to `-` for unprivileged callers. `fetch()` paces every request, backs off exponentially to ten minutes,
and retries twenty times. Pages already on disk are skipped unless `--force`, so an
interrupted run resumes instead of starting over, and one page failing no longer
takes the other two down with it. Expect minutes, not seconds.

## Deliberately not done

- **`docs/luckysevens.html` is untouched.** Repointing the three `href`s at the
  local copies would fix the dead links, but that edits the exhibit and was not
  asked for. Say the word and it is a three-line follow-up. My suggestion if you
  want it: keep the visible text naming the original URL — that is the citation —
  and point the `href` at the archived copy, which is the convention for citing
  something that has gone offline.
- Nothing decides whether these should be **publicly served**. They land under
  `docs/`, which GitHub Pages publishes, so merging this makes them reachable.
  That is probably what you want for a citation, but it is your call and it is
  reversible.
- Local browser caches were checked first and are a dead end: Chrome and Firefox
  hold nothing, Arc has only tab-state records of the forum root and the
  Audio_Review index (last opened 2024-08-28), never the three threads. Safari's
  `History.db` is unreadable without Full Disk Access — worth granting if you want
  the visit record, though history holds URLs, not page bodies.

